{
  "abstract": "Class-group exponent bounds obtained from an ordinary Rédei matrix can improve when higher pairings have positive rank, but an unfinished prime prefix does not determine the entire pairing. We give an explicit prefix bound and determine its exact strength when one prime remains. On the common positive kernel of each prefix diagonal, generalized Rédei reciprocity supplies a fixed alternating form $S(a,b)=[a,b,ab]$. Completing the prefix by $\\ell$ odd primes removes at most $\\ell-1$ kernel dimensions, yielding a computable exponent-dividing-$16$ class-number capacity. The dyadic coordinate belongs to this positive space precisely for prime discriminant $+8$. For one variable prime in a fixed ordinary-matrix and modulo-$8$ cell, we identify all conic Kummer relations: they are the alternating tensors on the intersection of the character and ideal-norm squareclass spaces. Consequently the exact minimum higher-pairing rank is half the rank of $S$, attained infinitely often by classical Chebotarev. This explicitly includes the intersecting case beyond the classical disjoint-space matrix distribution. Primitive-conic relative and absolute evaluators cover overlapping labels, cyclic quartic entries and dyadic factors. Controls include sixty certified fields, 270 pairing entries and 3,304 exact square decisions checked by two author implementations. A sharp example gives class number at most $128$ for exponent-dividing-$16$ fields of discriminant $-8\\cdot5\\cdot41p$. Existing large pilot trees show no gain. No new governing-field theorem, census, effective first-prime estimate or global discriminant bound is claimed.",
